It adapts files from OSS Scanner (https://github.com/anthropics/oss-scanner), Copyright 2026 Anthropic PBC,
licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0. Each adapted file keeps OSS Scanner's copyright and SPDX lines
where it had them, and says what was changed:

  README.md, CONTRIBUTING.md, SECURITY.md, CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md
  templates/project.yaml, templates/Dockerfile, templates/patch_style.md (from its templates/threat_model.md)
  tools/validate.py, tools/check, tools/check_build.py
  .github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md, .github/CODEOWNERS, .github/dependabot.yml
  .github/workflows/validate.yaml, .github/workflows/build.yaml, .github/workflows/triage.yaml,
  .github/workflows/terms.yaml, .github/scripts/triage.py

CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md is the Contributor Covenant, version 2.0 (https://www.contributor-covenant.org).

infra/base-builder/ takes its driver (driver/), its compiler wrappers and flags (toolchain.sh), the shape of a
fuzz target's link (link-fuzz-target), the release's recipes (recipes.sh) and the steps of its build around a target
(release-target.sh) from the build recipe of oss-fuzz-theorems release 2026.10.0-rc.2
(https://github.com/theorem-labs/oss-fuzz-theorems), Copyright 2026 Theorem Labs, PBC.

The files tools/port.py writes under projects/<name>/ and tools/seeds/<name>/ come from that same build recipe: the
pins in build.sh, a target's own files, and the build-only patches. Where such a file holds a project's own text (a
copy of its harness that the release edited, or a patch that writes a file generated from the project's sources),
that text keeps the project's licence; the recipe's UPSTREAM-CARRIED.tsv names each, with its origin and licence.

A file under projects/<name>/ copied from a project's own repository keeps that project's licence, which the file
names in its header.
